pub fn find_all(text: &str, pattern: &str) -> Vec<usize> {
    let text: Vec<char> = text.chars().collect();
    let pattern: Vec<char> = pattern.chars().collect();
    if pattern.is_empty() || pattern.len() > text.len() {
        return Vec::new();
    }
    // lps[i] = length of the longest proper prefix of pattern[..=i]
    // that is also a suffix of it.
    let mut lps = vec![0; pattern.len()];
    let mut len = 0;
    for i in 1..pattern.len() {
        while len > 0 && pattern[i] != pattern[len] {
            len = lps[len - 1];
        }
        if pattern[i] == pattern[len] {
            len += 1;
        }
        lps[i] = len;
    }
    let mut matches = Vec::new();
    let mut j = 0;   // length of the current partial match
    for (i, ch) in text.iter().enumerate() {
        while j > 0 && *ch != pattern[j] {
            j = lps[j - 1];
        }
        if *ch == pattern[j] {
            j += 1;
        }
        if j == pattern.len() {
            matches.push(i + 1 - pattern.len());
            j = lps[j - 1];   // keep going: occurrences may overlap
        }
    }
    matches
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **Char indices:** both strings are collected into `Vec<char>` up front, so every index in the algorithm is a char index and multi-byte characters are single elements. The cost is one O(n + m) pass of extra memory, the standard trade for index-correctness in Rust.
- **Overlaps:** after a full match, `j` falls back to `lps[j - 1]` instead of 0, so searching for "aaa" in "aaaaa" finds indices 0, 1, and 2.
- **Linear time:** the text pointer `i` never moves backwards; mismatches only shorten the partial match via the LPS table. Table construction uses the same fallback loop, keeping the whole algorithm O(n + m).
- **Edge cases:** empty pattern and pattern-longer-than-text return early with an empty vector, before the table build would index into an empty pattern.

