pub struct UserInput {
    pub name: String,
    pub email: String,
    pub age: i64,
    pub password: String,
}

pub struct ValidUser {
    pub name: String,
    pub email: String,
    pub age: i64,
}

#[derive(Debug)]
pub struct FieldError {
    pub field: &'static str,
    pub message: String,
}

pub fn validate_user(input: &UserInput) -> Result<ValidUser, Vec<FieldError>> {
    let mut errors = Vec::new();

    let name = input.name.trim();
    if name.is_empty() {
        errors.push(FieldError { field: "name", message: "must not be empty".into() });
    } else if name.chars().count() > 50 {
        errors.push(FieldError {
            field: "name",
            message: "must be at most 50 characters".into(),
        });
    }

    let email = input.email.trim();
    let parts: Vec<&str> = email.split('@').collect();
    if parts.len() != 2 || parts[0].is_empty() || !parts[1].contains('.') {
        errors.push(FieldError {
            field: "email",
            message: "must look like name@domain.tld".into(),
        });
    }

    if !(13..=120).contains(&input.age) {
        errors.push(FieldError {
            field: "age",
            message: "must be between 13 and 120".into(),
        });
    }

    let pw = &input.password;
    if pw.chars().count() < 10
        || !pw.chars().any(|c| c.is_ascii_digit())
        || !pw.chars().any(|c| c.is_alphabetic())
    {
        errors.push(FieldError {
            field: "password",
            message: "must be 10+ characters with a letter and a digit".into(),
        });
    }

    if errors.is_empty() {
        Ok(ValidUser { name: name.to_string(), email: email.to_string(), age: input.age })
    } else {
        Err(errors)
    }
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **All errors, fixed order:** each field's check runs unconditionally and pushes into one Vec in name → email → age → password sequence; the function only decides Ok vs. Err at the very end, so a caller gets the complete problem list in a stable order suitable for form rendering.
- **Character counts, not bytes:** name length and password length use `chars().count()`, so a 50-character name with multi-byte characters isn't rejected for its byte length.
- **Email shape:** splitting on '@' and requiring exactly two parts rejects both missing and doubled @ signs in one test; the local part must be non-empty and the domain must contain a dot.
- **Password policy as one error:** the three password sub-rules produce a single FieldError — the spec asks for one message per field, and listing which sub-rule failed would leak policy detail into a field-level message the caller may display verbatim.

```rust
pub struct UnionFind {
    parent: Vec<usize>,
    size: Vec<usize>,
}

impl UnionFind {
    pub fn new(n: usize) -> Self {
        UnionFind { parent: (0..n).collect(), size: vec![1; n] }
    }

    pub fn find(&mut self, mut x: usize) -> usize {
        let mut root = x;
        while self.parent[root] != root {
            root = self.parent[root];
        }
        // Second pass: point every node on the path straight at the root.
        while self.parent[x] != root {
            let next = self.parent[x];
            self.parent[x] = root;
            x = next;
        }
        root
    }

    pub fn union(&mut self, a: usize, b: usize) -> bool {
        let (mut ra, mut rb) = (self.find(a), self.find(b));
        if ra == rb {
            return false;
        }
        if self.size[ra] < self.size[rb] {
            std::mem::swap(&mut ra, &mut rb);
        }
        self.parent[rb] = ra;
        self.size[ra] += self.size[rb];
        true
    }

    pub fn connected(&mut self, a: usize, b: usize) -> bool {
        self.find(a) == self.find(b)
    }
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **Path compression:** `find` is iterative in two passes — walk to the root, then rewind the path pointing each node directly at it. (The recursive one-liner version risks stack depth on long chains; this has no such limit.) Because compression mutates, `find` and therefore `connected` take `&mut self`, which the signatures reflect.
- **Union by size:** the smaller root is attached under the larger, with `size` maintained only on roots — after a root is demoted its stale size is never read again, since `size` is only consulted on values returned by `find`.
- **Merge reporting:** `union` returns false exactly when the roots already agree, which is the signal cycle-detection callers (e.g. Kruskal's algorithm) need.
- **Amortized cost:** compression plus size-balanced trees give the classic inverse-Ackermann bound in practice.

