    capacity_milli: u64,
    tokens_milli: u64,
    refill_milli_per_sec: u64,
    last_sec: u64,
}

impl TokenBucket {
    pub fn new(capacity_tokens: u64, refill_tokens_per_sec: u64, now_sec: u64) -> Self {
        TokenBucket {
            capacity_milli: capacity_tokens * 1000,
            tokens_milli: capacity_tokens * 1000,
            refill_milli_per_sec: refill_tokens_per_sec * 1000,
            last_sec: now_sec,
        }
    }

    pub fn allow(&mut self, now_sec: u64, cost_milli: u64) -> bool {
        let elapsed = now_sec.saturating_sub(self.last_sec);
        self.last_sec = now_sec;
        let added = elapsed.saturating_mul(self.refill_milli_per_sec);
        self.tokens_milli = (self.tokens_milli + added).min(self.capacity_milli);
        if self.tokens_milli >= cost_milli {
            self.tokens_milli -= cost_milli;
            true
        } else {
            false
        }
    }

    pub fn balance_milli(&self) -> u64 {
        self.tokens_milli
    }
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **Integer-only accounting:** scaling by 1000 at construction moves the fractional boundary into the integer domain — a refill of 1 token/sec is 1000 milli-tokens/sec, and a request can cost, say, 250 milli-tokens for a quarter-token operation. No float ever appears, so behavior is identical on every platform.
- **Refill on every call:** the refill block runs before the grant check regardless of outcome, and `last_sec` always advances — a denied caller doesn't bank un-claimed elapsed time for later.
- **Clock safety:** `saturating_sub` makes a repeated or out-of-order timestamp yield zero elapsed instead of underflowing (debug panic) or wrapping (release), and `saturating_mul` keeps absurd elapsed values from overflowing before the capacity cap applies.
- **Cap:** the `min` after refill enforces capacity on the *balance*, not on the addition, so a long idle period still results in exactly one full bucket.

```rust
pub struct SortedBag<T> {
    items: Vec<T>,
}

impl<T: Ord> SortedBag<T> {
    pub fn new() -> Self {
        SortedBag { items: Vec::new() }
    }

    pub fn insert(&mut self, value: T) {
        let pos = self.items.partition_point(|x| x < &value);
        self.items.insert(pos, value);
    }

    pub fn count(&self, value: &T) -> usize {
        let lo = self.items.partition_point(|x| x < value);
        let hi = self.items.partition_point(|x| x <= value);
        hi - lo
    }

    pub fn remove_one(&mut self, value: &T) -> bool {
        let pos = self.items.partition_point(|x| x < value);
        if pos < self.items.len() && &self.items[pos] == value {
            self.items.remove(pos);
            true
        } else {
            false
        }
    }

    pub fn min(&self) -> Option<&T> {
        self.items.first()
    }

    pub fn max(&self) -> Option<&T> {
        self.items.last()
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.items.len()
    }

    pub fn iter(&self) -> std::slice::Iter<'_, T> {
        self.items.iter()
    }
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **Binary search everywhere:** `partition_point` finds the lower bound (`first index where x >= value`) and the upper bound (`first index where x > value`); `count` is the distance between the two bounds, so duplicates are counted without scanning them individually beyond the searches themselves.
- **Insert position:** inserting at the lower bound keeps the Vec sorted and places a new copy before existing equals — any position inside the equal run would satisfy the spec, but the lower bound is the one `remove_one` and `count` already compute.
- **remove_one:** it verifies the element at the lower bound actually equals the target before removing — essential, because the lower bound of an absent value points at the next-larger element.
- **Honest complexity:** searches are O(log n); the Vec insert/remove itself is O(n) due to shifting, which is the known trade of a sorted-array bag versus a balanced tree — fine for small, cache-friendly collections.

```rust
use std::collections::HashMap;
use std::hash::Hash;

struct Entry<V> {
    value: V,
    expires_at: u64,
    seq: u64,
}

pub struct TtlCache<K, V> {
    capacity: usize,
    seq: u64,
    entries: HashMap<K, Entry<V>>,
}

impl<K: Eq + Hash + Clone, V> TtlCache<K, V> {
    pub fn new(capacity: usize) -> Self {
        assert!(capacity > 0, "capacity must be positive");
        TtlCache { capacity, seq: 0, entries: HashMap::new() }
    }

    pub fn get(&mut self, key: &K, now: u64) -> Option<&V> {
        let expired = match self.entries.get(key) {
            None => return None,
            Some(e) => e.expires_at <= now,
        };
        if expired {
            self.entries.remove(key);
            return None;
        }
        self.entries.get(key).map(|e| &e.value)
    }

    pub fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V, expires_at: u64, now: u64) {
        self.entries.retain(|_, e| e.expires_at > now);
        if !self.entries.contains_key(&key) && self.entries.len() >= self.capacity {
            let victim = self.entries.iter()
                .min_by_key(|(_, e)| (e.expires_at, e.seq))
                .map(|(k, _)| k.clone())
                .unwrap();
            self.entries.remove(&victim);
        }
        self.seq += 1;
        let seq = self.seq;
        self.entries.insert(key, Entry { value, expires_at, seq });
    }

    pub fn len(&self) -> usize {
        self.entries.len()
    }
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **Purge before evict:** `put` first retains only entries with `expires_at > now`, so a live entry is never evicted while expired ones are still occupying space.
- **Eviction key:** `min_by_key((expires_at, seq))` — earliest expiry is the primary criterion and the insertion sequence breaks ties deterministically toward the oldest-inserted entry. Re-putting refreshes `seq`, so a renewed entry is treated as newly inserted for tie-breaking, as specified.
- **Expiry boundary:** the comparison is `expires_at <= now` in both `get` and the purge — an entry is dead *at* its expiry tick, consistently in both paths.
- **Borrow discipline in get:** the expired check is computed into a bool first, ending the immutable borrow before `remove` needs a mutable one — the standard two-phase pattern for map-entry expiry.

