```typescript
function searchRotated(nums: number[], target: number): number {
  let lo = 0;
  let hi = nums.length - 1;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (nums[mid] === target) return mid;
    if (nums[lo] <= nums[mid]) {
      // Left half [lo, mid] is sorted.
      if (nums[lo] <= target && target < nums[mid]) {
        hi = mid - 1;
      } else {
        lo = mid + 1;
      }
    } else {
      // Right half [mid, hi] is sorted.
      if (nums[mid] < target && target <= nums[hi]) {
        lo = mid + 1;
      } else {
        hi = mid - 1;
      }
    }
  }
  return -1;
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **One sorted half, always:** in a rotated array with no duplicates, at least one of [lo, mid] or [mid, hi] is a clean ascending run. `nums[lo] <= nums[mid]` identifies the left run (equality only when lo == mid, a one-element run, which is trivially sorted).
- **Containment test per half:** if the target lies within the sorted half's value range, search there; otherwise it must be in the other half — the rotation point — so the window jumps there. Either way the window at least halves, giving O(log n).
- **Boundaries:** target equal to nums[mid] is caught before the branch logic; the range tests use `< nums[mid]` / `> nums[mid]` on the inner side so the already-checked midpoint isn't re-included, and the outer ends are inclusive because those elements haven't been ruled out yet.
- **No-rotation case:** an unrotated array just takes the left-sorted branch every time, degrading gracefully to ordinary binary search.

