class UndoManager<T> {
  private undoStack: T[] = [];
  private redoStack: T[] = [];
  private current: T;

  constructor(initial: T, private capacity: number) {
    if (capacity <= 0) throw new Error("capacity must be positive");
    this.current = initial;
  }

  get state(): T {
    return this.current;
  }

  get canUndo(): boolean {
    return this.undoStack.length > 0;
  }

  get canRedo(): boolean {
    return this.redoStack.length > 0;
  }

  execute(next: T): void {
    this.undoStack.push(this.current);
    if (this.undoStack.length > this.capacity) {
      this.undoStack.shift();
    }
    this.redoStack = [];
    this.current = next;
  }

  undo(): boolean {
    const previous = this.undoStack.pop();
    if (previous === undefined) return false;
    this.redoStack.push(this.current);
    this.current = previous;
    return true;
  }

  redo(): boolean {
    const next = this.redoStack.pop();
    if (next === undefined) return false;
    this.undoStack.push(this.current);
    this.current = next;
    return true;
  }
}
```

How each spec clause is met:

- **Redo invalidation:** `execute` clears the redo stack unconditionally — once a new action branches off, the old future no longer exists. Forgetting this is the canonical undo bug (redo resurrecting a state from an abandoned timeline).
- **Capacity on the undo stack:** the push-then-shift pattern drops the oldest retained state exactly when the stack would exceed capacity, so at most `capacity` undos are ever possible. Redo doesn't need its own cap: it can never hold more than the undos that produced it.
- **Boolean protocol:** undo/redo return false on an empty stack and leave `current` untouched — callers can wire them straight to button handlers and refresh `canUndo`/`canRedo` for disabled states.
- **Immutability assumption:** states are stored by reference with no cloning, per the spec — the manager is O(1) per operation, and correctness depends on callers producing a new state value per execute rather than mutating in place.

